---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import WorkflowCanvas from '../components/WorkflowCanvas.astro';
import BeforeAfter from '../components/BeforeAfter.astro';
import ConnectedSystems from '../components/ConnectedSystems.astro';
import WorkflowGallery from '../components/WorkflowGallery.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import Testimonials from '../components/Testimonials.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import founderPhoto from '../assets/images/niraj.jpg';
import { services, faqs, site, founder, fitFor, pricing, engagementTerms, engagementNote, testimonials } from '../data/site';
import { getWork } from '../lib/work';

const work = await getWork();

const schema = [
  {
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    '@id': `${site.domain}/#faq`,
    mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
    })),
  },
  /*
    Reviews attached to the organization. Deliberately no `aggregateRating` —
    LinkedIn recommendations carry no star rating, so inventing an average out
    of them would be fabricated structured data. Individual reviews are the
    honest representation and are what search engines can actually corroborate
    against the linked profiles.

    Filtered to paying clients. A `Review` node asserts a customer
    relationship, so emitting one for a former colleague would be a claim the
    site cannot support — and it is the exact failure this comment block exists
    to prevent. His quote still appears on the page as ordinary text.
  */
  ...testimonials
    .filter((t) => t.isClient)
    .map((t) => ({
      '@type': 'Review',
      itemReviewed: { '@id': `${site.domain}/#organization` },
      author: { '@type': 'Person', name: t.name, url: t.linkedin },
      reviewBody: t.quote,
    })),
];
---

<Base schema={schema}>
  <!-- Hero: a point of view, not a feature list.

       No eyebrow above the headline. The category label ("workflow automation
       agency") still carries in the <title>, the meta description and the
       sub-line below, so search and social lose nothing — and the page now
       opens on the claim instead of on a label. "Part of oneProductLab" was
       also raising a question it didn't answer for anyone who hasn't heard of
       the parent; it remains in the footer, where a curious reader looks.

       Rejected headlines worth remembering. "Most companies run on copy-paste"
       described the reader's own operation as amateurish in the first seven
       words — and the reader is usually the person who built those processes.
       "The work between your systems. Handled." needed decoding, and "handled"
       is a shrug rather than a promise.

       This frames the purchase as the trade a buyer is actually weighing:
       capacity without cost. "Overhead" is deliberate — it is a line in a
       budget, where softer words like "busywork" shrink the problem and
       quietly argue the price down. -->
  <!--
    Two columns above 1024px: the claim on the left, a system visibly running on
    the right. The board is the reason the hero changed — an agency that sells
    invisible work has to show the work in the first screen, or it is asking the
    reader to take the whole proposition on faith.
  -->
  <section class="wrap pt-10 pb-6 md:pt-14 md:pb-8">
    <div class="grid items-center gap-12 lg:grid-cols-[0.95fr_1.05fr] lg:gap-14">
      <div>
        <h1 class="text-[2.8rem] leading-[1.02] sm:text-[3.6rem] lg:text-[4.1rem]">
          Grow the business.{' '}
          <span class="text-[var(--color-signal-ink)]">Not the overhead.</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-xl text-lg text-[var(--color-ink-2)]">
          The tools you already pay for, wired into one system that runs the
          repetitive work by itself — and it’s yours outright.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
          <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-primary">Book a free call</a>
          {/*
            A second door out of the hero, pointing at the price.

            This sent people to the work. Case studies are proof, but they are
            not the question a stranger has thirty seconds in — that question is
            "can I afford this", and it is the one most agency sites refuse to
            answer. Sending an unready visitor to a published number is the
            strongest thing this site can do with them: it is the differentiator,
            it disqualifies fast, and it costs the reader nothing. The work is
            still one click away in the nav and three sections down the page.
          */}
          <a href="/pricing/" class="btn btn-ghost">See what it costs</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="lg:pl-2">
        <WorkflowCanvas />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!--
    The problem, first thing after the hero.

    Client proof used to sit here. It has moved down to just after the price,
    where doubt actually peaks — praise before relevance reads as noise, because
    a stranger is still asking "do these people solve *my* problem" rather than
    "can I trust them". This also lifts the problem and service copy above the
    fold-adjacent area, which is the keyword-bearing content worth ranking.

    The problem is drawn rather than described. The qualification list that used
    to live in its own full section is folded in here, because "is this my
    problem?" and "am I the right buyer?" are the same question asked twice.
  -->
  <section class="border-y border-[var(--color-line)] bg-[var(--color-paper-2)]">
    <div class="wrap py-16 md:py-20">
    {/*
      Near-even columns rather than 0.85/1.15.

      The old split left the text column at ~430px, which was too narrow for
      the qualification lines — "data" and "platform" each fell to a second
      line on their own, and a one-word orphan line reads as a mistake. The
      text column now gets roughly the same width as the diagram, which is
      enough for those lines to close up, and it also brings the two columns
      to a similar height so neither hangs below the other.

      Text left, diagram right — the same way round as the hero above it.

      Mirroring this section was tried as the fix for readers calling the two
      screens repetitive, and it was worse: the eye has to restart on the right
      after two sections of starting on the left, and the reversed row read as
      a different template rather than a continuation. The repetition is
      answered by the surface instead. This block now sits on the tinted paper
      with a rule top and bottom, so it is plainly a second section rather than
      more of the first, and the reading rhythm is left undisturbed.
    */}
    <div class="grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_1.05fr] lg:items-center lg:gap-14">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 max-w-md text-3xl md:text-4xl">
          Your business shouldn’t depend on repetitive work.
        </h2>
        {/*
          The cost, as a worked example rather than a claim.

          This began life as a bare "~$12,000/year" — a round, research-shaped
          number with nothing behind it. That is the one kind of copy this site
          cannot afford: a page arguing for fixed prices and honest scoping
          loses more from one unsupported statistic than it gains from the
          statistic landing.

          Two things fix it. The label says "worked example", so the figure is
          framed as arithmetic rather than a finding about the reader's
          business. And the inputs are printed, so it stops being our number
          and becomes theirs — anyone can substitute their own salary and get
          their own answer, which persuades better than a figure we chose and
          cannot be wrong in a way that costs trust.

          Deliberately conservative: base salary only, no employer taxes,
          benefits or seats, and no error costs. The true figure is higher.
        */}
        <div class="mt-6 max-w-sm border-l-2 border-[var(--color-signal)] pl-4">
          <p class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-[0.1em] text-[var(--color-ink-3)]">
            Worked example
          </p>
          <p class="price mt-1 text-[var(--color-ink)]">~$12,000<span class="text-[0.5em] text-[var(--color-ink-3)]">/year</span></p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-[0.92rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-ink-2)]">
            One person, one day a week, re-typing between systems. It never
            shows up as a line item, so it rarely gets fixed.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-2 font-[var(--font-mono)] text-[0.7rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-ink-3)]">
            $55,000 salary ÷ 230 working days × 48 days. Use your own numbers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <ul class="reveal mt-8 border-t border-[var(--color-line-2)]">
          {
            fitFor.signals.map((s, i) => (
              <li
                data-stagger
                style={`--i:${i}`}
                class="flex gap-3 border-b border-[var(--color-line-2)] py-3.5"
              >
                <span class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm text-[var(--color-signal-ink)]" aria-hidden="true">
                  ✓
                </span>
                <span class="text-[0.95rem] text-[var(--color-ink-2)]">{s}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>

        {/*
          Closing line for the signals above.

          The two "not for you if" exclusions used to sit here. They failed the
          section's own question: this block asks "is this my problem?", and
          they answer "are these the right people?" — a question about us,
          settled by clauses about our staffing and a freelancer's rate. Under
          an eyebrow reading THE PROBLEM, a reader hits them and has to work out
          why the subject changed. They now sit next to the price, where "is
          this the right size of job for these people" is the question actually
          being asked.
        */}
        <p class="mt-5 font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-ink-3)]">
          Sound familiar? That’s the work we take out.
        </p>
      </div>

      {/*
        No `self-start`: the diagram was pinned to the top of a taller text
        column, which left it floating with a block of empty space beneath it.
        Centred against the text, the two columns read as one row.
      */}
      <div class="reveal">
        <BeforeAfter />
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!--
    What we build — and what it gets wired into.

    "Your stack" used to run as its own full section here: a heading, a claim
    that you don't need another app, the exclusions, and this diagram. It was
    ~745px to explain tooling, which is not what a visitor is deciding at this
    point in the page. The section is gone; its three parts were rehomed to
    where each already had a job. The exclusions joined the signals list above.
    The claim survives as the line under this heading — it is really an
    argument about what we build, so it reads better as this section's premise
    than as a section of its own. And the diagram, which was the only part
    doing work no sentence could, is now the visual for this section instead of
    illustrating a paragraph that repeated it.
  -->
  <section class="wrap py-16 md:py-20">
    {/*
      Two columns at `lg`, not `md`.

      At `md` this split the row at 768px, which handed the diagram a ~340px
      column. The ring sizes its nodes off the stage width and its own stacking
      fallback is keyed to the viewport, not the column — so between 768px and
      1024px the outer nodes had a container too narrow to sit inside and spilled
      past the viewport edge, scrolling the whole page sideways. `lg` matches the
      `min-width: 64rem` the component already uses to grow its stage, so the
      row now splits exactly when the diagram has the width to be in a row.
    */}
    <div class="grid items-center gap-12 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_0.95fr]">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">What we build</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl md:text-4xl">Where we usually remove manual work.</h2>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-md text-[var(--color-ink-2)]">
          You don’t need another app. You need the tools you already pay for to
          talk to each other, in the right order, without a person in the middle.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
          <a href="/services/" class="btn btn-ghost">All services</a>
          <a href="/services/integrations/" class="btn btn-ghost">How integrations work</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal">
        <ConnectedSystems />
      </div>
    </div>
    <!--
      A two-column matrix rather than six full-width rows. This section is a
      directory — every item leads somewhere else — so it earns scanning speed,
      not scrolling. Full-width rows gave each of six short lines the same
      vertical weight as a real content section, and pushed the proof and
      pricing below it further down the page.

      `gap-px` over a line-coloured background draws the dividers, so the grid
      keeps hairline rules in both axes without per-cell border bookkeeping.
    -->
    <ul class="reveal mt-10 grid gap-px border-y border-[var(--color-line)] bg-[var(--color-line)] md:grid-cols-2">
      {
        services.map((s, i) => (
          <li data-stagger style={`--i:${i}`} class="bg-[var(--color-paper)]">
            <a
              href={`/services/${s.slug}/`}
              class="disclose-host group flex h-full gap-4 py-5 pr-4 transition-colors md:px-6 hover:bg-[var(--color-paper-2)]"
            >
              <span class="mt-1 font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs text-[var(--color-ink-3)]">{s.index}</span>
              <div class="min-w-0">
                <h3 class="font-[var(--font-display)] text-lg transition-colors group-hover:text-[var(--color-signal-ink)]">
                  {s.title}
                  {/*
                    Travels with the last word instead of being pinned to the
                    far right, where a full-width row left it stranded metres
                    from the label it belongs to.
                  */}
                  <span
                    class="ml-1 inline-block font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm text-[var(--color-ink-3)] transition-transform group-hover:translate-x-1 group-hover:text-[var(--color-signal-ink)]"
                    aria-hidden="true"
                    >→</span
                  >
                </h3>
                <div class="disclose">
                  <div>
                    <p class="mt-1 text-[0.92rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-ink-2)]">{s.brief}</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <!--
      Says out loud that the list is a sample rather than the full offer, Without this a
      reader whose problem isn't one of the six assumes we don't do it and
      leaves — the exact opposite of what a services list is for.Phrases as an invitation
      rather than a denial.
    -->
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-xl font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-ink-3)]">
      Whatever’s slowing your team down, it’s still worth a call.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!--
    Workflow patterns, directly after the service categories.

    The list above names six categories; this shows what one actually looks
    like, which is the difference between a reader understanding the offer and
    recognising their own problem in it. Placed before the work section on
    purpose: patterns are illustrative and belong next to the categories they
    illustrate, while the section below is evidence and should not have to
    share a reader's attention with hypotheticals.

    Every card links to a service page that already exists, so this adds
    internal links and the phrases buyers search without creating a single new
    URL to thin out.
  -->
  <WorkflowGallery />

  <!-- Proof -->
  {/*
    `inverse-ink`, not `paper`.

    `--color-slate` is a fixed dark surface: it stays dark in both themes. But
    `--color-paper` is a *flipping* token — it is light in the light theme and
    near-black in the dark theme. Pairing the two meant that in dark mode this
    section rendered #17150f text on a #0b0a07 background: a contrast ratio of
    1.08, i.e. the entire section was invisible. The inverse scale exists for
    exactly this — it is light in both themes, because the surface it describes
    is dark in both themes.
  */}
  <section class="bg-[var(--color-slate)] text-[var(--color-inverse-ink)]">
    <div class="wrap py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="flex items-end justify-between gap-6 flex-wrap">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow text-[var(--color-inverse-ink-3)]">Selected work</p>
          <h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl md:text-4xl">Systems we’ve built, in the open.</h2>
        </div>
        <a
          href="/work/"
          class="btn border-[var(--color-inverse-line)] text-[var(--color-inverse-ink)] transition-colors hover:bg-[var(--color-inverse-ink)] hover:text-[var(--color-slate)]"
          >All work</a
        >
      </div>
      <!--
        Structured proof cards.

        These replaced a plain title-and-summary row, and they absorbed the job
        the deleted metrics strip used to do. The reader now gets the outcome
        figures from actual projects rather than backend statistics from a
        previous job: index, honest kind badge, sector, title, summary, then the
        sourced result metrics from the case study itself.

        Discrete cards on a gap, matching the pricing tiers. They previously
        drew their dividers with `gap-px` over a lighter background, which is
        the same table effect the pricing section had: three separate projects
        reading as three columns of one object, with every height difference
        looking like a fault rather than a fact about the write-ups.
      -->
      <ul class="reveal mt-10 grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-3">
        {
          work.slice(0, 3).map((item, i) => (
            <li data-stagger style={`--i:${i}`} class="card card--dark">
              {/*
                The link fills the card and carries no surface of its own: the
                hover treatment now belongs to `.card--dark`, and a second
                background change here would fight it.
              */}
              <a
                href={`/work/${item.id}/`}
                class="group flex h-full flex-col p-7"
              >
                <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-3">
                  <span class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs tracking-[0.14em] text-[var(--color-inverse-ink-4)]">
                    CASE // {String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}
                  </span>
                  {/*
                    The honest label, given badge treatment rather than being
                    hidden in a run of muted text. A reader who spots "internal build"
                    for themselves trusts the two marked "client" more.
                  */}
                  <span
                    class={`font-[var(--font-mono)] text-[0.62rem] uppercase tracking-[0.12em] border px-2 py-0.5 ${
                      item.data.kind === 'client'
                        ? 'border-[var(--color-signal-bright)] text-[var(--color-signal-bright)]'
                        : 'border-[var(--color-inverse-line)] text-[var(--color-inverse-ink-3)]'
                    }`}
                  >
                    {item.data.kind.replace('-', ' ')}
                  </span>
                </div>

                <p class="mt-5 border-t border-[var(--color-inverse-line)] pt-5 font-[var(--font-mono)] text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-[0.12em] text-[var(--color-inverse-ink-3)]">
                  {item.data.sector}
                </p>
                <h3 class="mt-2 font-[var(--font-display)] text-xl transition-colors group-hover:text-[var(--color-signal-bright)]">
                  {item.data.title}
                </h3>
                <p class="mt-3 text-[0.92rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-inverse-ink-2)]">
                  {item.data.summary}
                </p>

                {/*
                  `flex-1` on the list is what keeps "Read the build" pinned to
                  the bottom of every card regardless of how much the summaries
                  above it differ in length.
                */}
                <ul class="mt-6 flex-1 space-y-2 border-t border-[var(--color-inverse-line)] pt-5">
                  {item.data.result.slice(0, 3).map((r) => (
                    <li class="flex gap-2.5 text-[0.85rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-inverse-ink-2)]">
                      <span class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-[var(--color-signal-bright)]" aria-hidden="true">
                        —
                      </span>
                      <span>
                        <strong class="font-[var(--font-mono)] font-medium text-[var(--color-inverse-ink)]">
                          {r.metric}
                        </strong>{' '}
                        {r.label}
                      </span>
                    </li>
                  ))}
                </ul>

                <span class="mt-6 inline-flex items-center gap-2 border-t border-[var(--color-inverse-line)] pt-5 font-[var(--font-mono)] text-sm text-[var(--color-signal-bright)]">
                  Read the build
                  <span class="transition-transform group-hover:translate-x-1" aria-hidden="true">
                    →
                  </span>
                </span>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-6 font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs text-[var(--color-inverse-ink-4)]">
        Every figure above is sourced on the case study it comes from.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!--
    "How we work" used to sit here: six steps, two rows, plus a founder byline.

    Removed deliberately. The homepage already earns the sale three other ways
    — the work, the price, the testimonials — and a process breakdown is
    something a reader wants *after* they are interested, not while they are
    deciding whether to be. It is the whole subject of /approach/, which is
    linked from the header on every page and from the closing block below, so
    nothing is orphaned and no keyword is lost that the destination page does
    not own more strongly.

    The founder moved to the closing block, where "who am I actually talking
    to" is the live question rather than a claim bolted onto a process list.
  -->

  <!--
    Pricing and risk reversal, on the homepage. These are the strongest arguments
    the agency has and they were previously a click away, which meant most
    visitors never met them.
  -->
  <section class="border-y border-[var(--color-line)] bg-[var(--color-paper-2)]">
    <div class="wrap py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-end justify-between gap-6">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">What it costs</p>
          {/*
            `max-w-md` wrapped this to two lines while most of the row sat
            empty. The heading is short enough to hold one line at every width
            the three-column grid below it survives, so the constraint only
            cost vertical space.
          */}
          <h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl md:text-4xl">Published prices. Fixed scope.</h2>
        </div>
        <a href="/pricing/" class="btn btn-ghost">Full pricing</a>
      </div>

      {/*
        Same card primitive as the pricing page, so a visitor who clicks
        through meets the component they just looked at rather than a
        differently-styled restatement of it.
      */}
      <ul class="reveal mt-10 grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-3">
        {
          pricing.map((tier, i) => (
            <li
              data-stagger
              style={`--i:${i}`}
              class="card p-6"
            >
              <p class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs text-[var(--color-ink-3)]">{tier.duration}</p>
              <h3 class="mt-2 font-[var(--font-display)] text-lg">{tier.name}</h3>
              <p class={`price mt-3 text-[var(--color-ink)] ${tier.priceValue === null ? 'price-text' : ''}`}>{tier.price}</p>
              <p class="mt-3 max-w-[34ch] text-[0.9rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-ink-2)]">
                {tier.summary}
              </p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      {/*
        One trust block, not two.

        This grid and the "founding clients" section that used to sit two
        screens below were both answering "why should I believe you" — six
        bordered statements in a row, which a reader pattern-matches after the
        first three and scrolls past. Merged to the four objections that
        actually arrive at a price, and kept here, adjacent to the number,
        because that is the moment the doubt forms.
      */}
      <ul class="reveal mt-10 grid gap-x-8 gap-y-6 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
        {
          engagementTerms.map((t, i) => (
            <li
              data-stagger
              style={`--i:${i}`}
              class="border-t border-[var(--color-line-2)] pt-4"
            >
              <p class="font-[var(--font-mono)] text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.1em] text-[var(--color-signal-ink)]">
                {t.title}
              </p>
              <p class="mt-2 text-[0.92rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-ink-2)]">{t.detail}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      <p class="mt-8 max-w-2xl text-[0.92rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-ink-3)]">
        {engagementNote}
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!--
    Client proof, immediately after the price.

    This is the moment doubt peaks: a reader has just seen what it costs and is
    asking whether it is worth it. A named person who paid and would say so
    publicly answers that better than any further explanation — which is why
    the pricing page uses the same order.

    Deliberately far from "Systems we've built" higher up. That section proves
    the work exists; this proves people were glad they paid for it. Adjacent,
    the two read as the same argument twice; four sections apart, they read as
    two different kinds of evidence.
  -->
  {/*
    `plain` because the pricing section directly above is already the tinted
    surface. Left on its default, this would sit on the same paper-2 and the
    two would run together into one undifferentiated block.
  */}
  <Testimonials plain />

  <!--
    The founding-client section that used to sit here has been merged into the
    pricing block above. Its terms now sit beside the price they justify, and
    the capacity line runs as prose under them. Nothing was cut but the second
    heading and the duplicated framing.
  -->

  <Faq
    items={faqs}
    note="What people ask before getting in touch. Cost is covered in more detail on the pricing page."
  />

<!--
    Closing CTA on the dark surface — compact, consistent with other sections.
  -->
  <section class="bg-[var(--color-slate)] text-[var(--color-inverse-ink)]">
    <div class="wrap py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-[1fr_auto] md:items-start md:gap-8">
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-[var(--font-display)] text-2xl md:text-3xl leading-snug text-balance">
            Tell us what’s still done by hand.
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-3 max-w-md text-[0.95rem] text-[var(--color-inverse-ink-2)]">
            One workflow, honestly described. We’ll map it and tell you whether it’s
            worth automating.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 md:ml-4">
          <a
            href="/contact/"
            class="btn border-[var(--color-inverse-ink)] bg-[var(--color-inverse-ink)] text-[var(--color-slate)] transition-colors hover:border-[var(--color-signal-bright)] hover:bg-[var(--color-signal-bright)] hover:text-[var(--color-inverse-ink)]"
            >Book a free call</a
          >
          <a
            href={`mailto:${site.email}`}
            class="btn border-[var(--color-inverse-line)] text-[var(--color-inverse-ink)] transition-colors hover:bg-[var(--color-slate-2)]"
            >Email us</a
          >
        </div>
      </div>

      {/*
        The founder, at the point of contact.

        "A real person replies within two hours" used to run as a standalone
        line opposite the buttons, which asked the reader to take the word
        "real" on trust. Naming and showing that person answers the question
        the block actually raises — who am I about to email — and carries the
        two internal links the removed process section used to hold.

        Full width under both columns rather than inside the left one. Confined
        to the text column it set a ~45-character measure and ran to three
        cramped lines while the entire right half of the band sat empty below
        the buttons. Across the full width it is two lines, and the rule above
        it now reads as closing the section rather than dividing one column.
      */}
      <div
        class="mt-10 flex items-center gap-4 border-t border-[var(--color-inverse-line)] pt-6"
      >
        <Image
          src={founderPhoto}
          alt=""
          width={96}
          height={96}
          loading="lazy"
          class="h-11 w-11 shrink-0 rounded-full border border-[var(--color-inverse-line)] object-cover"
        />
        <p class="max-w-3xl text-[0.92rem] leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-inverse-ink-2)]">
          <strong class="font-medium text-[var(--color-inverse-ink)]">{founder.name}</strong>{' '}
          reads every enquiry himself and replies within two hours. He writes the
          code as well as the proposal —{' '}
          <a href="/approach/" class="link-underline link-underline--inverse"
            >how an engagement runs</a
          >{' '}
          and{' '}
          <a href="/about/" class="link-underline link-underline--inverse"
            >about the agency</a
          >.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
